Format
description: a named constraint on node content - not a type, never a decoder; markups, media, structures
- >
  A **format** is a **named constraint** on a node's content - finer than any type can state:
  `type: string` admits every string, while `format: text/x-plantuml` claims the string parses as a
  PlantUML diagram. A format refines a facet; it is **not a type**
  ([terminology](*::language:logical-graph:terminology)), and it has an open sub-lattice of its own (whether
  `text/markdown` subtypes `text/plain` is deliberately unsettled). And it is **not a decoder**:
  since the concrete/format split, how bytes or text become the value is the
  [concrete](*::language:concretes)'s job - the format only says what the decoded value *means*.
  The `format:` schema keyword is the [meta chapter](*::meta:format)'s subject.
- 'A few formats in action, spanning the range - JSON Schema''s own values first, then beyond them:'
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  adopted:  !!<format: date-time> 2019-06-01        # JSON Schema's own …
  contact:  !!<format: email> cat@example.org
  homepage: !!<format: uri> https://whiskers.example
  note:     !!<format: text/markdown> |             # … a markup, rendered by its view …
    Likes **laser pointers**.
  diagram:  !!<format: text/x-plantuml> |           # … text with a dedicated renderer …
    @startuml
    Cat -> Ball : chase
    @enduml
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  # … and, in a directory's .yo/meta.yo, the format CONSTRAINS while the concrete DECODES:
  members:
    avatar: {type: binary, format: image/png}       # bytes ARE the value; the format names the meaning
    weight: {concrete: binary/int32/le}             # the CONCRETE decodes four bytes to an integer
- >
  Usually a format constrains a **string scalar**. But a scalar is just a node **without
  members** ([members](*::language:logical-graph:members)), so nothing limits formats to strings: a format
  may name the shape of *any* node - `x-yamlover-chapter` constrains a whole omni node (a title
  self value, a `description` key, a positional body), and the platform's tag, fragment, and
  annotation structures are formats over keyed nodes.
- 'The formats the platform knows today:'
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - Format
    - JSON Schema
    - Usually constrains
    - What it names
      width: 2
  - - '`text/marklower`'
    - '-'
    - a string
    - 'The default prose markup - what a bare string with no stated format is read as.'
  - - '`text/markdown` · `text/asciidoc` · `text/html` · `text/x-latex`'
    - '`contentMediaType`'
    - a string
    - 'Foreign text markups, rendered by their markup views.'
  - - '`text/x-plantuml` · `text/csv` · `text/tab-separated-values`'
    - '`contentMediaType`'
    - a string
    - 'Text with a dedicated renderer - a diagram, a table.'
  - - '`text/plain`'
    - '`contentMediaType`'
    - bytes
    - 'Deliberately opaque - kept as raw bytes so the client decodes under a chosen encoding (legacy Cyrillic text files are common), rather than the server fixing UTF-8. Declaring a charset CONCRETE (`text/utf-8`, `text/ibm866`) decodes it server-side instead.'
  - - '`text/x-yamlover` · `text/x-yaml` · `text/x-json5p`'
    - '-'
    - a string
    - 'Quoted code - the surfaces kept *as text*, unparsed (how this book shows its examples).'
  - - '`text/javascript` · `text/css` · `text/x-python` · `text/x-sql` · `text/xml` · `application/x-ndjson`'
    - '`contentMediaType`'
    - a string
    - 'FOREIGN source code - a language this project does not speak, named so it is never mistaken for a broken `.yo`: held verbatim, never parsed, never prose-link-scanned, shown by the plaintext view.'
  - - '`image/png` · `application/pdf` · any MIME type'
    - '`contentMediaType`'
    - bytes
    - 'Opaque media - the viewer picks a renderer by the MIME name; an open set.'
  - - '`date-time`'
    - '`format: date-time`'
    - a string
    - 'A value format kept from JSON Schema.'
  - - '`x-yamlover-chapter`'
    - '-'
    - an omni node
    - 'A book chapter - title self value, `description`, positional body; the format that shapes this book.'
  - - '`x-yamlover-onto` · `x-yamlover-fragment`'
    - '-'
    - an omni or keyed node
    - 'The platform structures - an onto and a fragment are both omni.'
  - - '`application/octet-stream`'
    - '`contentMediaType`'
    - bytes
    - 'The fallback - a binary leaf nothing else claimed.'
- >
  Two families that USED to sit in this table are formats no longer - they are
  [concretes](*::language:concretes), because they name a *decoding*, not a constraint: the
  **sub-document encodings** (`format: yamlover`, `json5p`, the `.../meta` variants → now
  `concrete: yamlover/stream`, `json5p/code`, `yamlover/meta`, …) and the **codecs**
  (`format: int32/le` → now `concrete: binary/int32/le`; base64 is `concrete: base64`, the tag
  `!!binary` its inline alias - [transforms](*::transform)). The old format spellings read
  forever; the authored corpus states the concrete.
